y":{"cache_read_tokens":0.0,"cache_write_tokens":0.0,"input_tokens":1774.857142857143,"llm_calls":1,"output_tokens":281.14285714285717,"requests":1},"prerequisite-consistency":{"cache_read_tokens":0.0,"cache_write_tokens":0.0,"input_tokens":8084.0,"llm_calls":1,"output_tokens":76.0,"requests":1},"project.portability":{"cache_read_tokens":0.0,"cache_write_tokens":0.0,"input_tokens":1774.857142857143,"llm_calls":1,"output_tokens":281.14285714285717,"requests":1},"project.symbol-reference-completeness":{"cache_read_tokens":0.0,"cache_write_tokens":0.0,"input_tokens":82771.0,"llm_calls":1,"output_tokens":2245.0,"requests":7}}}},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Establish (and state in the Approach) that combining contiguous _RENDER blocks into one pandoc document yields output equivalent to per-block rendering — either extend the run-level preservation check beyond code_fragments to detect cross-block prose reflow, or bound the batch so blocks that can reparse against neighbours (setext underlines, reference-link defs, list continuations, blank-separated-but-not-isolated paragraphs) are not merged.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.2889,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Landed _render_unit renders one unit per pandoc document (wiki_render.py:369-370, 376-378), so blocks never share a parse context","code_fragments only extracts fence/indented/inline-code content (wiki_render.py:266-288) — the lifted run-level check inherits that scope and is blind to prose changes","Plan: 'This story groups each maximal run of contiguous _RENDER units into ONE pandoc document'","Plan asserts blanks preserve structure ('re-emitting their exact text as the blank lines between blocks') but does not argue pandoc's per-block output is byte-identical when blocks are concatenated"],"finding":"Batching a maximal run of contiguous _RENDER units into ONE pandoc commonmark document is not semantically equivalent to the landed per-unit rendering: pandoc parses block boundaries relative to surrounding content, so adjacent blocks (even separated by re-emitted blank lines) can reparse differently than in isolation (lazy paragraph continuation, setext-header underlines, reference-link definitions, list continuation). The plan's safety net — the run-level preservation check lifted from _render_unit — only verifies code_fragments survive (wiki_render.py:376-390, 266-288); it cannot detect prose-level cross-block reflow/merge, so batching can silently corrupt prose that the landed one-document-per-block design structurally prevented.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9ebc43ab185c5415","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'Batch contiguous SAFE units to bound subprocess cost' / 'run-level preservation check'","norm_id":"n8aa1f3aa86e1f1cb","priority":0.8889,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","scenarios":["A run where block A ends without a trailing blank and pandoc treats block B's first line as a lazy continuation of A's paragraph","A block whose first line is `=`/`-` runs and would be read as a setext underline of the preceding block's last line","Two adjacent blocks where the second is a reference-link definition that pandoc now resolves against a link in the first"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify that test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back's fixture spawns a process that forks a surviving pipe-holding grandchild, and assert that grandchild PID is dead after reaping — so the test exercises the killpg path, not just direct-child timeout.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5b","T5e","T7","T9"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.075,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'asserts the group is reaped'","approach: 'a jira-reader spinning at ~95.8% CPU (a pipe-holding grandchild) survives (bug d843, bpo-30154)'","VERIFY-COMMAND DEFECT TAXONOMY item (4) fixture invalidity: the fixture must actually exercise the grandchild-survival claim"],"finding":"The timeout AC's proving test test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back 'asserts the group is reaped', but the plan does not specify that its fixture actually spawns a pipe-holding GRANDCHILD — the exact case (bpo-30154 / bug d843) that distinguishes reap_process_group from a plain subprocess.run(timeout=). A fixture whose pandoc stand-in only stalls the direct child would pass the assertion without ever exercising the killpg path the AC exists to prove.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1893a5161800ed07","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, line 1 (test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back)","norm_id":"n7b2c7b880f10e8a9","priority":0.875,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["Fixture times out a leaf pandoc process with no children; test goes green but the killpg grandchild-reaping code path never ran."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Have the timeout fixture invoke a stand-in that double-forks / spawns a sleeping grandchild holding the stdout pipe, capture its PID, and assert os.kill(grandchild_pid, 0) raises ProcessLookupError after fallback.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.875,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Re-verify pandoc 3.6.1's persistent-server capability against the correct surface (the `pandoc server` subcommand and/or bundled `pandoc-server` executable, per pandoc's MANUAL), and either justify batching over a persistent server on accurate grounds or reconsider the daemon approach for the spawn-cost target.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.0389,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'exposes no --server/daemon — verified absent from --help'","Retrieved pandoc.org MANUAL.html: 'Use pandoc server to spin up a local web server, and have your application make requests to it. The server runs in sandboxed mode...'","Retrieved github.com/jgm/pandoc doc/pandoc-server.md: 'pandoc-server is a web server that can perform pandoc conversions. It can be used either as a running server or as a CGI program.'","pandoc-server manpage (manpagez.com/man/1/pandoc-server): documents the standalone pandoc-server(1) tool","The persistent server is the established alternative that most directly addresses the plan's own headline problem (the flat ~0.3s/field spawn cost, ~20 min serial reconcile)"],"finding":"The plan dismisses a persistent pandoc server ('out of scope') by asserting pandoc 3.6.1 'exposes no --server/daemon — verified absent from --help', but pandoc's persistent server is exposed as the `pandoc server` subcommand / separate `pandoc-server` executable, not a `--help` flag — so the capability check targets the wrong surface and does not actually establish the alternative's absence.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f970e5a9c8845f2c8","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section — parenthetical: 'The pinned wheel's pandoc 3.6.1 exposes no --server/daemon — verified absent from --help — so a persistent server is out of scope.'","norm_id":"n804b9d098222b036","priority":0.6611,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A reviewer or future maintainer asks why batching was chosen over a persistent conversion server that would eliminate per-field spawn cost entirely","The pinned pandoc actually ships `pandoc server` and the spawn-cost problem could be solved more directly, making the batching approach an under-justified partial fix"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Replace the '--help'-based dismissal with an accurate prior-art comparison: note that pandoc DOES offer a persistent server (`pandoc server` subcommand / `pandoc-server` executable per pandoc's MANUAL), state whether the pinned wheel bundles that entrypoint, and justify batching vs. a daemon on real trade-offs (e.g. sandbox restrictions, jira-reader hang isolation, process-lifecycle complexity) rather than on the absence of a `--server` flag.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7778,"verification":{"binary":{"